// The Sumi diagnostics registry.
//
// This file is the one declaration of every diagnostic code the compiler
// reports. `cargo xtask codegen` regenerates from it:
//
//   crates/frontend/src/generated/codes.rs  the `syntax` group's constants
//   crates/hir/src/generated/codes.rs       the `semantic` group's constants
//   docs/reference/generated/diagnostics.md the diagnostics chapter: every
//                                           code, its explanation, and the
//                                           corpus case that shows it
//
// CI runs `cargo xtask codegen --check` and fails when any of them drifts.
// Every code must be shown by a case under `tests/corpus/`, which
// `crates/hir/tests/codes.rs` checks; the chapter takes its example from
// the case that shows the code with the fewest other diagnostics.
//
// One declaration per line. `//` starts a comment; `///` lines document
// the declaration below them, here, in the generated Rust, and in the
// chapter. A code's spelling is `group/name`, both kebab-case, and it is
// stable: a code is never renamed or reused for something else.
//
//   group name    a namespace of related codes; the codes after it belong
//                 to it until the next group
//   code name     one class of diagnostic

/// Reported by the frontend: what the lexer rejects, where the parser
/// recovers, and the layout rules the parser checks. Every one is an
/// error. The tree is still built around it, and a fix is attached where
/// the repair is mechanical.
group syntax

/// A string literal reaches the end of its line without a closing `"`. The
/// literal ends at the line break, so nothing after that line is affected.
code unterminated-string
/// A carriage return not followed by a line feed. A line ends with `\n` or
/// `\r\n`.
code lone-carriage-return
/// A character with no meaning in Sumi source outside a string or comment.
/// Names are ASCII letters, digits, and `_`.
code unknown-character
/// Identifier characters attached to an integer literal, as in `1u32`,
/// `1e5`, or `1_000`. Literals take no suffix, exponent, or separator.
code unknown-suffix
/// An integer literal with leading zeros, as in `007`. The fix removes
/// them.
code noncanonical-number
/// A backslash in a string literal beginning none of the escapes `\n`,
/// `\r`, `\t`, `\\`, `\"`, and `\0`.
code unknown-escape
/// Punctuation with no role in the language, such as `;`, `[`, or `@`.
code unknown-punctuation

/// Something other than a function item at the top level of the file.
code expected-item
/// A token that cannot begin a statement where a block's next statement
/// should start.
code expected-statement
/// An expression is required, after an operator, `=`, or `(` for instance,
/// and the next token cannot begin one.
code expected-expression
/// The name a `fn`, `let`, or parameter declares is missing.
code expected-name
/// A type is required after `:` or `->` and none follows.
code expected-type
/// One particular token is required and the message names it: a closing
/// bracket, a `,` between list elements, or the `(` of a parameter list.
/// For a missing closer, a label points at the opener and the fix inserts
/// the closer.
code expected-token
/// A function signature is followed by neither a block nor `=` and an
/// expression.
code expected-body
/// Two statements share a line. A line break ends a statement; there is no
/// `;`.
code expected-boundary
/// A token inside an expression that neither continues nor ends it. The
/// parser skips to where it can resume and labels what it skipped.
code unexpected-syntax
/// Expressions nest deeper than the parser's limit of 256 levels, which
/// keeps parsing on a bounded stack.
code nesting-too-deep
/// A binary operator glued to an operand, as in `a+b` or `a<b`. Binary
/// operators are spaced on both sides; glued, `<` opens type arguments and
/// `*` and `&` are reserved for prefix operators. The fix inserts the
/// spaces.
code unspaced-binary-operator
/// A prefix operator separated from its operand, as in `- x`. Prefix
/// operators are glued; the fix removes the space.
code spaced-prefix-operator
/// A space between a function name or callee and its `(`. The fix removes
/// it.
code spaced-list-opener
/// A function item's name on the line after its `fn`. The fix moves the
/// name onto the `fn` line.
code function-name-on-next-line
/// A function item beginning on the line where the previous one ended. The
/// fix moves it onto a line of its own.
code function-item-on-same-line
/// A binding's name on the line after its `let`. The fix moves the name
/// onto the `let` line.
code binding-name-on-next-line
/// Comparisons chained, as in `a < b < c`. A comparison yields a boolean
/// that no comparison accepts; write two comparisons joined by `&&`.
code chained-comparison

/// Reported by semantic checking over every function whose syntax is
/// complete: names, the scalar types `int`, `bool`, and `unit`, and calls.
/// A function with a syntax error in its body is not checked, and every
/// code here is an error.
group semantic

/// A type reference naming none of `int`, `bool`, and `unit`.
code unknown-type
/// A name with no declaration in scope: no parameter or binding for a
/// value, no function item for a call.
code unknown-name
/// Two function items of one file, or two parameters of one function, with
/// the same name. A label points at the first.
code duplicate-name
/// A call whose callee is a parameter or binding. Only function items are
/// callable, since every local holds a scalar.
code not-callable
/// A call passing a different number of arguments than the function
/// declares parameters. A label points at the declaration.
code arity
/// An expression of one type where another is required: an operand, a
/// condition, an initializer against its annotation, an argument, a
/// result against its return type, or `if` branches that disagree.
code type-mismatch
/// A statement's expression, other than a block's last, has a value that
/// is not unit. Discard it with `_ =` to keep it.
code unused-value
/// A function without a return type whose result cannot be determined: its
/// result is used as two types, or nothing fixes it. Add a return type
/// annotation.
code cannot-infer
/// An integer literal outside the signed 64-bit range.
code integer-range
/// A construct scalar checking does not handle yet, such as a closure, a
/// string literal, or a call through anything but a function name. The
/// function is left unchecked.
code unsupported
